<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamphaeng Phet</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chakangrao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamphaeng Phet is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ping-river">Ping River</a> in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, known in the fourteenth century as Chakangrao. A fortified frontier town of the Sukhothai kingdom, it was besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in the 1370s and later became an Ayutthayan provincial centre. Its walls and temple ruins were inscribed with those of Sukhothai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamphaeng-phet.jpg" alt="Kamphaeng Phet" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Adirach Toumlamoon. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author an... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamphaeng_Phet_City_Pillar_Shrine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
